\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Facilitator:
\f1\b0  Before we dive into questions, we\'92ll quickly set the container. The conversation will take around 30\'9640 minutes; it could run longer, but we\'92ll track time. If you\'92re ever in a rush or uncomfortable, tell us and we\'92ll wrap up. You choose the depth\'97share as much or as little as you want. Everything you share stays between us. Any question can be skipped.\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Perfect. Thank you. You create a very safe container\'97your unique energies make a warm bubble. It\'92s beautiful.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  Thank you. We\'92re aiming to build a product with that same feeling\'97like the three of us holding space together.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  Let\'92s start with some life context. How old are you, where do you live, what\'92s your current life situation? What are you prioritizing now\'97work, personal growth, relationships?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  I\'92m 24\'97turning 25 in a few days\'97and I\'92m living in Hawaii. I\'92m from California and spent about a year in Asia, where I met two of you. My priority is always self-growth and expansion. Right now, my priority is work. I\'92d like my priority to be more about coming back to my heart and body, but at the moment I\'92m dancing between the \'93Matrix\'94 world\'97this system I don\'92t love that pressures me about money and survival\'97and returning to myself. It\'92s a chapter: I\'92m spending energy where I don\'92t necessarily want to, while trying to make space to return to my heart.\
As for relationships, I\'92m not romantically connecting with anyone\'97just platonic friendships. When I filled out the survey I was in that space, feeling a fresh anxious attachment with someone. Even now, thinking about it, I feel it in my body. These days I\'92m working hard and figuring out how to build something like what you\'92re building\'97helping women and humans\'97while also making money, because that matters. My real goal is to help people (and myself) feel freer, more expansive, authentic. And also earn a living in this human world. So I\'92m asking: how do I bring all of that together?\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  You were traveling before\'97are you settling in Hawaii for a while?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Yes. I needed to be in one place. My nervous system was exhausted from traveling. I needed home\'97rooting, grounding, community\'97and I needed to make money. These islands are magnetic, profound, healing\'97the nature is mind-blowing. I was traveling; now I\'92m settling.\
What\'92s interesting is that I\'92m usually the one who wants to run\'97adventure, go, experience\'97which is amazing but also has a shadow of escapism. I\'92m scared of rooting even though my body needs it. So being here is a practice: \'93I\'92m staying.\'94 No looking up flights. At least for the next six months, I\'92ll be here. Somatically, that on-edge, looking-around feeling is similar to anxious attachment\'97versus being grounded and rooted, something feels like it\'92s pulling me away.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  And our product is for all seasons\'97not only when someone is \'93in it.\'94 Sometimes it hits like a hurricane; other times there\'92s a quieter integration phase.\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Exactly. There are seasons of a lot of connection\'97community, loverships\'97and seasons of not connecting or integrating. Your product could really help in the \'93not actively in connection\'94 season\'97the stability phase, strengthening the muscle so next time I connect I\'92m more aware and can come back to myself. That\'92s when I\'92d actually want to use it.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  How long have you known about attachment styles? When did you begin to recognize anxious attachment in yourself?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  I learned about attachment styles in therapy around age 20\'97about five years ago\'97but I didn\'92t start recognizing it in real time until about three years ago. At first they were just words I\'92d heard. Later I began to notice, \'93Oh, this is what\'92s happening in my body and mind\'97this is my anxious attachment.\'94 Even then, I didn\'92t always know how to work with it.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  How often do you experience it, and how intense does it get?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  It depends on closeness, context, and how safe I feel, but it can get very intense and spiral quickly. I experience it in most connections. There was one connection in the last year where I didn\'92t\'97but I\'92m not sure how open my heart was there. When I\'92m truly open and vulnerable, intensity goes up because I feel more exposed. If I have more walls up, I\'92m protecting myself from going to that anxious place; it can feel like opening guarantees anxiety.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  When was the last time?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Today. Technically I\'92m \'93not in relationship,\'94 but you\'92re always in relationship with yourself\'97and even with a place. I notice a kind of anxious attachment with the island. And there\'92s a guy I\'92ve known for four years in California\'97major crush. He texted me about a month ago; we\'92ve been casually in touch as friends, but I\'92m attracted to him. Even without a defined relationship, it shows up: \'93He hasn\'92t texted back.\'94 Not a full spiral, but the sensation is there.\
When I really like someone\'97someone I admire and who lights up parts of me that trigger my shadow (\'93Am I good enough? Could I be rejected?\'94)\'97then a late text can activate the pattern. It\'92s a dose of it.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  How do you feel it in your body\'97what are your indicators?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  My heart races; it feels like a trauma response. My mind floods; I can\'92t think clearly. I\'92m not present or anchored in my body; I feel flighty, uprooted. It\'92s overwhelming and all-consuming\'97a tornado. I feel powerless, like a little girl. No control. That\'92s the most frustrating part\'97no matter how aware I am, I want to rip my hair out. I forget who I am.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  How do you usually deal with it?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Honestly, nothing has fully \'93sedated\'94 it when I\'92m deep in it. I journal\'97free write, scribble\'97let it out. That helps some, but it comes back. I try movement\'97sweat, get in my body, feel strong. Talking to friends helps, but sometimes it reinforces the story; I can still feel alone in it, plus there\'92s shame and embarrassment\'97like an addict chasing the \'93hit\'94 when they text back and the relief rushes in. There\'92s even a sneaky part of me that likes the high.\
What would help is something to listen to\'97auditory is big for me. A voice that says: \'93What you\'92re feeling is normal. You\'92re not crazy. You\'92re not alone.\'94 Take the shame out, then guide me back into my body. I\'92m very body-based. Safety first, then somatic regulation.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  That aligns with our idea\'97SOS practices for acute moments, and deeper, slower journeys for quieter seasons.\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Yes. Safety is most important: a grounded presence, almost maternal. I prefer women\'92s voices in healing contexts; I feel safer. I don\'92t need heavy science lectures when I\'92m dysregulated\'97energy-based, feeling-based guidance lands better for me.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  Structure vs. freedom\'97what works better?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Steps help\'97a map. Suggested sequence with the option to jump to what I need (e.g., go straight to Step 5). When I\'92m dysregulated, I don\'92t trust myself or feel grounded; having a pre-written path I can just follow is really supportive\'97while keeping flexibility.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  Anything that would turn you off?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Nothing specific comes to mind right now; I\'92ll tell you if something does. The voice and felt safety matter more than anything.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  Would you use simple delivery\'97say, links via email to a browser-based experience (vs. an app)?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Yes. If the email clearly says \'93Click here,\'94 with simple login steps, that\'92s workable. Maybe ask users to flag or pin the email so it\'92s easy to find in a crisis.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  Where do you usually find practices\'97do you buy courses, follow creators?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Mostly through friends\'97EFT tapping with a friend, vocal toning via a friend\'92s app I have access to. I also follow people on Instagram for masterclasses and workshops\'97both live (nice for shared energy and accountability) and recorded (nice for privacy and timing). Recorded can reduce accountability, but it\'92s convenient.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  If you saw an Instagram ad for our SOS pack, what\'92s a comfortable price point to try?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  It depends\'97if I\'92m really struggling, I\'92m willing to spend meaningfully on something that helps. A sample would be great so I can feel resonance and effectiveness. If it lands, I\'92d pay for ongoing access.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  If you tried a short version and it really worked, what kind of follow-up would you be interested in\'97something deeper, longer, maybe a guided journey?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Yes, something deeper, definitely. When I find something that actually works, I want to go all in. But I\'92d need it to feel like real work, not fluff. I want to feel that I\'92m actually processing, integrating, transforming\'97not just relaxing for a few minutes.\
It could be a mix: short SOS-type tools for emergencies, and then longer journeys for when you\'92re ready to go deeper. Like a ceremony you prepare for.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  What kind of facilitation voice resonates with you in those longer sessions\'97gentle and soft, or more direct and structured?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Depends on the mood, but I usually prefer a bit of both: grounded but powerful. Not too dreamy, not too \'93woo.\'94 Something that feels safe but also challenges me. Sometimes I need someone to lovingly call me out. I respond to authenticity\'97the voice should sound human, not like a meditation robot.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  And how do you feel about music in such sessions?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Music is huge for me. It can carry the whole journey. I like when it\'92s thoughtfully chosen\'97like the breath and the music are talking to each other. The music helps me feel, helps me cry or shake something out.\
If you find the right music, the body just follows. I like drum beats, grounding bass, something tribal or elemental. But I also love silence at the end\'97a moment to integrate.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  You\'92ve clearly explored many practices already. Could you describe how you first came to breathwork\'97what drew you in?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  My first experience was through a friend who was a therapist. She mentioned holotropic breathwork. I was curious and organized my first session just to see what it was like. At first, I didn\'92t breathe\'97I acted as a sitter. Then I tried it myself, and it blew my mind.\
That was ten years ago. Since then, I\'92ve done multiple holotropic sessions, and also softer practices. I love the emotional release, but I also know that deep breathwork isn\'92t for everyone\'97it can be too intense. Some people shouldn\'92t go that far without preparation.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  And for you personally\'97what\'92s the most valuable thing breathwork gives?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Connection. To my body, to life, to truth. It helps me stop spinning in my head. When I breathe, I remember who I am. It\'92s a reset.\
It\'92s also empowering\'97because in the end, you\'92re healing yourself. You\'92re not waiting for someone to fix you. It\'92s your breath, your power. That\'92s incredible.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  Do you ever use breathwork spontaneously in daily life\'97say, when you\'92re triggered or anxious?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  All the time. I\'92ve trained my body to recognize when I need it. Sometimes I just take a few deep breaths, sometimes I tap my body and say, \'93I\'92m here.\'94 I reconnect with the body first, then start breathing.\
When you\'92re really triggered, it\'92s too late to start thinking \'93I should breathe now.\'94 You need to build that muscle in calmer moments. Practice it every day, even when you\'92re fine\'97so that in crisis your body remembers.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  That\'92s beautiful. And which breath techniques work best for you in those situations?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Simple ones. Square breathing\'97inhale, hold, exhale, hold. Or long exhales to calm the nervous system. I also use grounding techniques\'97touching my legs, tapping, repeating \'93I\'92m here.\'94 It brings me back from dissociation.\
When I\'92m really gone, I can\'92t even breathe intentionally\'97so grounding comes first, then breath.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  Sounds like you already have a whole \'93emergency kit\'94 of your own.\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Exactly! That\'92s why your idea of an SOS kit resonates. It\'92s like having those tools ready in one place, with a guiding voice to remind you what to do.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  What about online breathwork experiences? Do you ever buy them or join live sessions?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  I\'92ve done a few. I prefer live for the sense of community, but I sometimes join online recordings too. The energy is different\'97it\'92s lonelier online\'97but still helpful. I like the flexibility of recordings, though the vibe is stronger live.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  What would make an online practice feel more connected for you?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Maybe if it felt like a shared ritual, even online. The guide could mention that others are breathing with you around the world\'97it gives a sense of togetherness. And of course, the voice and tone matter a lot.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  What kind of marketing or presentation would turn you off immediately?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Anything that promises instant transformation\'97\'93this will change your life in one session.\'94 No. That\'92s fake. Real work takes time and consistency.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  And what kind of messaging would draw you in?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Something honest: \'93This practice will help you connect to yourself in the moment.\'94 Or \'93It won\'92t fix everything, but it\'92ll support you.\'94 I like grounded truth.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  Would you describe yourself as more \'93scientifically minded\'94 or \'93energetically/spiritually oriented\'94 when it comes to these practices?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  A balance of both. I love understanding how things work\'97what\'92s happening in the nervous system\'97but I also love the mystery. Science explains the mechanics; energy explains the meaning. They complement each other.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  What makes you trust a facilitator or decide to join a session with someone?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Personal recommendation. If someone I trust says, \'93You should try this,\'94 that\'92s everything. Community validation means a lot to me. I need to feel that the person leading knows what they\'92re doing, and that their energy is clean.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  That makes total sense.\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  And also, I like to see who else joins. The crowd matters. The kind of people a space attracts says a lot about the facilitator and the vibe.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  Yes, that\'92s such a good point.\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  I can tell a lot about a product by who\'92s drawn to it. I want to feel aligned with the energy of the people participating.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  You mentioned that you\'92d be willing to pay a good amount if the practice really helps. Could you share what \'93a good amount\'94 means for you\'97just approximately?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Hmm, it depends on what I\'92m getting. If it\'92s a full program, something I can use again and again\'97like a full journey\'97I\'92d easily pay around 
\f0\b $100\'96150
\f1\b0 .\
If it\'92s a smaller product, like an SOS kit, maybe 
\f0\b $30\'9650
\f1\b0  feels right. It\'92s important that it\'92s not just one-time use; I\'92d want to return to it, have access when I need it.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  That\'92s very helpful. And what makes you feel you\'92re getting value for that price?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Depth. Authenticity. When I can feel that someone really put heart and experience into it, not just repackaged generic stuff. I want to feel that it was made for real humans going through real emotions.\
I think it\'92s about how it 
\f2\i feels
\f1\i0 , not how many videos it has.\

\f0\b Facilitator:
\f1\b0  Beautifully said. And if you saw such a product in an Instagram ad, for example\'97what would make you click?\

\f0\b Participant:
\f1\b0  Honestly, energy. The tone of the message. I\'92d click if it feels like it\'92s written by someone who 
\f2\i gets it
\f1\i0 , not selling to me. Something like:\
